{"schema_version":1,"id":"018f0000-0000-7000-8000-00000000c473","stream":{"kind":"session","id":"01a06ee5-ad0e-7750-9534-32e3571b58b2"},"sequence":148,"recorded_at":1780531400000291,"record_type":"event","durability":"durable","causation_id":"268a8b73-3d5a-46cf-996a-bd1fafa2d2a2","payload_type":"task.lifecycle.output","payload_schema_version":1,"payload":{"kind":"task_lifecycle","command_id":"268a8b73-3d5a-46cf-996a-bd1fafa2d2a2","run_stream":{"kind":"run","id":"268a8b73-3d5a-46cf-996a-bd1fafa2d2a2"},"task_stream":{"kind":"task","id":"75086c89-537f-4a49-b48e-40c11f88c5ab"},"task_id":"75086c89-537f-4a49-b48e-40c11f88c5ab","event":{"kind":"output","task_id":"75086c89-537f-4a49-b48e-40c11f88c5ab","chunk":"Read text file `/tmp/rev/TASK-260830-1zvmw7_review-verdict.md`.\n1|# TASK-260830-1zvmw7 — Review Verdict (round 1, CR rev 1)\n2|\n3|**Verdict: CHANGES REQUESTED.** Route to `to-dev`.\n4|\n5|Scope reviewed: `git diff 9481a20..f81e2ea`, 6 paths, +3896/-2. Working tree at\n6|review time byte-identical to the CR candidate before and after every probe\n7|(`shasum -a 256` over all six package `.go` files, diffed against a pre-review\n8|baseline). Every mutant below was grep-confirmed present, compiled, run, and\n9|reverted; `manifest.go` sha256 re-checked as\n10|`d5a7b8cccf9670f98eb6e85cdbe76226fc8a293685177f685e0111fa74c7ab85` after each\n11|battery.\n12|\n13|Baseline accepted from the run, not re-derived: `go build ./...`, `go vet ./...`,\n14|`gofmt -l .` all exit 0; `go test ./...` green across 14 packages; package\n15|coverage 87.1%.\n16|\n17|---\n18|\n19|## Headline ratios\n20|\n21|| Measure | Ratio |\n22|| --- | ---: |\n23|| Mutants killed by the shipped suite | **64 / 96** |\n24|| Production refusal arms executed by the shipped suite | **71 / 121** |\n25|| Leaf-2 refusal assertions that check which arm fired | **0 / 29** |\n26|| G-A: exported paths probed for interior aliasing | 12 / 12 entry points, **1 injected aliasing bug survives** |\n27|\n28|---\n29|\n30|## G-A — leaf 1's pinned claim over the widened surface\n31|\n32|Method (same as leaf 1: enumerate, then probe — not read). `go doc -all` gives\n33|leaf 2 a new exported surface of 9 funcs, 5 types, 26 constants. I wrote a\n34|white-box probe that:\n35|\n36|1. snapshots the package interior — both built-in `Registration` records from a\n37|   fresh `New(\"2.1.0\", …)` and every package-level closed table\n38|   (`capabilityRegistry`, `operationVocabulary`, `evidenceRequirementVocabulary`,\n39|   `evidenceFactVocabulary`, `requirementFact`, the three `*Members` lists);\n40|2. calls **every** leaf-2 exported entry point that returns composite data —\n41|   `CapabilitiesForOperation` over all 10 operations, `ParseManifest`,\n42|   `ParseProbe`, `ParseEvidence`, `Reconcile`, `Admitted.Has`,\n43|   `Admitted.HasOperation`, `CheckOperation`, `UnsignedEvidenceBytes`,\n44|   `Registry.AdmitProbe`, `Registry.Resolve`;\n45|3. reflectively scribbles `REVIEW-POISON` into every reachable slice element,\n46|   map value, and settable string/bool/byte field of every returned value;\n47|4. re-snapshots and re-admits.\n48|\n49|**At HEAD the probe passes: no exported path reaches the built-ins or the closed\n50|tables.** The claim holds for the production code as written.\n51|\n52|**Finding G-A-1 (blocking, evidence).** The probe is not vacuous — but the\n53|shipped suite is. Positive control: change `parseClaim`'s return from the parsed\n54|slices to the registry row's slices\n55|\n56|```go\n57|DependentOperations:  row.dependentOperations,\n58|EvidenceRequirements: row.evidenceRequirements,\n59|```\n60|\n61|My probe kills it and shows `REVIEW-POISON` landing inside\n62|`capabilityRegistry[\"graceful_stop\"]` and `[\"headless_creation\"]`. **The shipped\n63|suite (52 test functions) passes with that mutant live.**\n64|`TestAdmittedSharesNoBackingArrays` mutates `probe.EvidenceIDs[0]`,\n65|`manifest.ProtocolVersions[0]` and `evidence[0].Facts[0]` — three fields, none of\n66|which can alias a package table — and then only asserts that `admitted` did not\n67|change; it never re-reads the interior and never re-admits.\n68|`Claim.DependentOperations` and `Claim.EvidenceRequirements` are the two exported\n69|slice fields on the whole new surface that *could* alias the closed registry, and\n70|neither is touched. Leaf 1's claim is therefore **re-established for 3 of the\n71|reachable composite fields and unestablished for the 2 that matter**.\n72|\n73|Failure scenario if it ever regresses: any caller doing\n74|`claim.DependentOperations[0] = \"attach\"` permanently rewrites the process-wide\n75|capability registry, changing which operations every later admission confers and\n76|which evidence proves them — and nothing in the suite notices.\n77|\n78|---\n79|\n80|## G-B — the traversal\n81|\n82|Rows discovered by walking `manifest.go` top to bottom, not by following the test\n83|file. 121 refusal arms; 96 mutants across every one of them; each row gets its\n84|mutant, its kill/survive, and a bucket.\n85|\n86|| # | Gate area (production site) | Mutants | Killed | Bucket |\n87|| --- | --- | ---: | ---: | --- |\n88|| 1 | Manifest closed schema — `parseManifestObject` | 7 | 4 | `partial` |\n89|| 2 | **Probe closed schema — `ParseProbe`** | 7 | **1** | **`sliver`** |\n90|| 3 | **Evidence closed schema — `ParseEvidence`** | 11 | 5 | **`partial`** |\n91|| 4 | Shared decode/member helpers | 10 | 5 | `partial` |\n92|| 5 | Identity recompute / JCS — `objectIdentity`, `checkIdentity` | 3 | 2 | `partial` |\n93|| 6 | Claim shape / registry-row binding — `parseClaim` | 3 | 2 | `partial` |\n94|| 7 | Generation digest — `GenerationDigest`, `checkProbeGeneration` | 4 | 4 | `full` |\n95|| 8 | Signed bytes — `UnsignedEvidenceBytes` | 20 | 19 | `full` (one stated bound) |\n96|| 9 | Signature verification — `checkEvidenceSignature`, nil verifier | 2 | 2 | `full` |\n97|| 10 | **Liveness / expiry — `checkEvidenceLiveness`** | 3 | 2 | **`partial`** |\n98|| 11 | **§4.B keyed claim relation — `checkClaimRelation`** | 3 | **1** | **`sliver`** |\n99|| 12 | Probe↔Manifest identity & membership | 4 | 3 | `partial` |\n100|| 13 | Evidence set / tuple / coverage / ID set | 12 | 11 | `partial` |\n101|| 14 | Operation gate — `CheckOperation`, `HasOperation` | 2 | 2 | `full` (but see F1 — the gate itself is wrong) |\n102|| 15 | **Registry-bound `AdmitProbe`** | 3 | **1** | **`sliver`** |\n103|| 16 | **Aliasing across the exported boundary (G-A)** | 1 | **0** | **`unevidenced`** |\n104|| | **Total** | **96** | **64** | |\n105|\n106|Cross-check from the other direction: a coverage profile over the shipped suite\n107|shows **50 of the 121 refusal arms are never executed at all**, and the\n108|never-executed set matches the survivor set area for area — the `sliver` rows\n109|above are not an artifact of mutant selection.\n110|\n111|### F1 (blocking, correctness — live spec violation)\n112|\n113|`CheckOperation(\"manifest\", …)` and `CheckOperation(\"probe\", …)` refuse\n114|unconditionally with `terminal_backend_capability_unproven`, **even when all 16\n115|registry capabilities are admitted** (verified by direct probe against a\n116|fully-populated `Admitted`).\n117|\n118|Two independent authorities say that is wrong:\n119|\n120|* the pinned spec's §4.D operation table gives both operations\n121|  `Capability dependencies: none`, and neither row's allowed-error list contains\n122|  `terminal_backend_capability_unproven`\n123|  (`manifest`: `protocol_incompatible, protocol_error, process_failed, timeout,\n124|  integrity_failure`; `probe`: `untrusted, manifest_probe_mismatch,\n125|  implementation_drift, protocol_error, process_failed, timeout,\n126|  integrity_failure`);\n127|* this file's own sibling function documents the opposite model —\n128|  `CapabilitiesForOperation`: *\"manifest and probe confer through no capability\n129|  and return an empty set: they carry no capability dependency.\"*\n130|\n131|Failure scenario: the lifecycle owner gates an adapter `manifest` call through\n132|`CheckOperation` — the operation this package admits into its own vocabulary —\n133|and the Manifest read can never proceed, refused with an error code the spec does\n134|not permit for that operation. No test covers either operation through\n135|`CheckOperation`; `TestCapabilitiesForOperation` checks both through the *other*\n136|function, where they behave as the spec says.\n137|\n138|### F2 (blocking, evidence) — four §4.B/§4.D rules have a named test that lands on the right arm but does not hold the gate\n139|\n140|At HEAD I confirmed each case reaches its intended clause. Under a mutant that\n141|removes the gate the case is named for, **the fixture is caught by a downstream\n142|arm instead and the suite stays green** — so the test does not fail when the gate\n143|admits what it must reject.\n144|\n145|| Named case in `TestReconcileRefusals` | Gate removed | Arm the case shifts to | Suite |\n146|| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n147|| `omitted manifest claim` | `checkClaimRelation` omission loop | `evidence claim binding` | green |\n148|| `static echo drift` | `probe static claim echo` equality | `evidence claim binding` | green |\n149|| `protocol not member` | `checkProbeMembership` protocol half | `evidence tuple binding` | green |\n150|| `evidence for false claim` | `!claim.Value` half of `checkEvidenceSet` | `evidence id set binding` | green |\n151|\n152|Root enabler: **0 of the 29 refusal assertions in `manifest_test.go` and\n153|`manifest_pin_test.go` check which arm fired.** All 29 assert only\n154|`IsMismatch(err)` / `IsStaleGeneration(err)` — and ~110 of the 121 arms return the\n155|same `CodeMismatch`. Leaf 1 did assert `refusal.Detail` where it mattered; leaf 2\n156|dropped that. Every one of these four fixtures over-determines the failure; each\n157|needs to be narrowed so only the named rule can reject it, and the assertion needs\n158|to name the clause.\n159|\n160|### F3 (blocking, evidence) — the untrusted-executable gate at the production entry point is unevidenced\n161|\n162|`checkManifestRecordBinding`'s executable-digest half\n163|\n164|```go\n165|if manifest.ExecutableDigest != record.ExecutableDigest {\n166|    return &Error{Code: CodeUntrusted, …, Detail: \"executable substitution\"}\n167|}\n168|```\n169|\n170|can be deleted whole and the suite stays green — no shift, no downstream arm, no\n171|test reaches it. This is the §6.5 \"trust established before any probe\" gate on\n172|`Registry.AdmitProbe`, the only registry-bound production entry point this leaf\n173|ships. `TestAdmitProbeExternalRealm` drives an external-kind backend end to end on\n174|the positive path; `TestAdmitProbeRefusesRecordDrift` covers the *version/kind/\n175|platform* half (`M36` is killed), leaving the digest half — the substitution case —\n176|positive-path-only.\n177|\n178|Failure scenario: a manifest whose `executable_digest` differs from the trusted\n179|registry record admits, and its probe's capabilities are activated, because the\n180|probe agrees with its own manifest (`checkProbeIdentity` compares probe↔manifest,\n181|not manifest↔record).\n182|\n183|### F4 (blocking, evidence) — expiry boundary proven on one side only\n184|\n185|`checkEvidenceLiveness` implements `observed_at <= now < expires_at`. At HEAD I\n186|confirmed all four boundary points behave correctly. But:\n187|\n188|* the lower bound has both sides covered (`future observed evidence` case, and\n189|  `M23` future-half deletion is killed);\n190|* the upper bound has only the far side. Narrowing `!now.Before(expires)` to\n191|  `now.After(expires)` — i.e. admitting evidence at exactly `expires_at` —\n192|  **survives**. `expired evidence` sets `expires_at` two months into the past; no\n193|  case sits at the instant.\n194|\n195|This is the shape this Story has already been burned by three times. It is one\n196|table row in the existing case list.\n197|\n198|### F5 (blocking, evidence) — the Probe and Evidence closed-schema gates are proven only for the Manifest\n199|\n200|The three parsers are independent code paths with their own literals. The Manifest\n201|parser's shape gates are well covered (`M85`–`M88` all killed).\n202|`TestProbeDocumentRefusals` (10 cases) and `TestEvidenceDocumentRefusals`\n203|(16 cases) omit the entire class the manifest test covers, and every one of these\n204|survives deletion:\n205|\n206|| Mutant | Gate deleted | Result |\n207|| --- | --- | --- |\n208|| M01 | `ParseProbe` schema URN | survives |\n209|| M02 | `ParseProbe` schema_version | survives |\n210|| M03 | `ParseProbe` `ParseID(backendID)` | survives |\n211|| M04 | `ParseProbe` `implementation_kind` | survives |\n212|| M05 | `ParseProbe` digest↔kind consistency | survives |\n213|| M06 | `ParseEvidence` schema URN | survives |\n214|| M07 | `ParseEvidence` schema_version | survives |\n215|| M08 | `ParseEvidence` `ParseID(backendID)` | survives |\n216|| M09 | `ParseEvidence` protocol major 1 | survives |\n217|| M10 | `ParseManifest` `implementation_kind` | survives |\n218|\n219|Failure scenario for M03/M08 specifically: `ParseID`'s reserved-namespace rule is\n220|the gate that stops a third party minting `ax.evil`. `TestManifestDocumentRefusals`\n221|has the `reserved backend id` case; Probe and Evidence have none, so the same rule\n222|could be dropped from two of three parsers unnoticed. This is the\n223|\"clause present in one context profile, absent from the other\" shape.\n224|\n225|### F6 (non-blocking, evidence) — six gates whose class is unevidenced\n226|\n227|Each survives; each is a one-line addition to an existing table-driven case list.\n228|\n229|| Mutant | Gate | Why the existing case does not hold it |\n230|| --- | --- | --- |\n231|| M41 | `checkSortedUnique` duplicate half (`>=`→`>`) | `duplicate evidence ids` appends the *smallest* ID, producing a descending pair caught by the ordering half. An adjacent duplicate is never built. |\n232|| M60 | `parseClaimList` duplicate half (`>=`→`>`) | `duplicate claims` appends `claims[0]`, same reason. |\n233|| M79 | `decodeCappedValue` depth cap | the `deep` case is 40 nested arrays; with the cap removed it is refused by `document shape` instead (top-level not an object). Assertion is arm-blind, so nothing changes. |\n234|| M81 | `decodeStrictObject` UTF-8 validity | Go's decoder substitutes U+FFFD, so identity recompute diverges and refuses anyway — the read-failure-vs-absence distinction this file's package doc claims is untested. |\n235|| M39 | `parseAttestationSignature` scheme prefix | `bad signature scheme` uses `\"sha256:abcd\"`, which fails base64 under the mutant. An unprefixed valid Base64 (`\"AA==\"`) is admitted by the mutant and refused at HEAD; that input is never built. |\n236|| M38 | `parseRealmMembers` required-members half for `credential_capable_execution_realm` | only the inverse (realm members on a plain claim) has a case. |\n237|\n238|### F7 (non-blocking, evidence) — four declared bounds never measured\n239|\n240|`M45` (`os_version` upper 256), `M89` (`platforms` non-empty), `M90` (16-claim\n241|cap), `M91` (256-evidence-ID cap) all survive. Leaf 1 measured its bounds at\n242|33-distinct/32-admission; leaf 2 declares four and measures none.\n243|\n244|### F8 (non-blocking) — two unreachable arms and one inaccurate doc\n245|\n246|* `checkProbeIdentity` L1550 `return mismatchf(\"probe manifest binding\")` is dead:\n247|  the enclosing `if` is reached only when the backend IDs are already equal (the\n248|  first `if` returned otherwise), so the inner `if` is always true. Never executed\n249|  in coverage.\n250|* `parsePlatformList` L950 `platforms[index-1] == platforms[index]` is subsumed by\n251|  the `>=` comparison two lines above. Never executed.\n252|* `checkEvidenceSet`'s doc says *\"Expired, wrong-generation, or dangling evidence\n253|  disables the claim it names\"*. The code refuses the whole reconciliation. Not a\n254|  behaviour bug — the code matches §4.B — but the comment describes a different\n255|  model.\n256|\n257|### F9 (blocking, DoD) — README carries a claim this leaf falsified, and no traceability binding was registered\n258|\n259|`README.md:1977` discharges nothing for clause `15.3#3` on the stated grounds that\n260|*\"this repository builds no RPC hello frame, no provider plugin, and **no\n261|TerminalBackend capability set**\"*. Leaf 2 ships exactly a TerminalBackend\n262|capability set: the closed 16-row `capabilityRegistry`, `Claim`, `Admitted`, and\n263|the emission of `terminal_backend_capability_unproven`. That sentence is now\n264|false.\n265|\n266|Leaf 1 updated `README.md` and registered its new negative arm in\n267|`internal/traceability/ownership.v0.5.0.json` with a re-pinned\n268|`reviewedOwnershipCanonicalSHA256`. Leaf 2 touched neither; `tracecheck` still\n269|reports `assigned_scopes=0`, `unevidenced=41`, `clauses_discharged=17/403` — no\n270|change for §4.B/§4.D/§6.5/§7.A. DoD item *\"README/doctor/capability evidence and\n271|specification traceability are updated without unsupported claims\"* is not met.\n272|\n273|---\n274|\n275|## What is good, stated so the rework does not undo it\n276|\n277|* **Signed-bytes canonicalization is the strongest area in the change: 19/20\n278|  narrowing mutants killed.** Dropping any single member from the\n279|  `UnsignedEvidenceBytes` object — `capability`, `backend_generation_digest`,\n280|  `expires_at`, `facts`, `issuer_id`, `platform`, `os_version`,\n281|  `conformance_fixture_id`, `issuer`, `protocol_version`, `terminal_backend_id`,\n282|  `observed_at`, and all five realm members — is caught, as is removing the domain\n283|  separator or its NUL. That is a genuine narrowing result, not a delete-only one.\n284|* Generation digest: 4/4, including domain removal *and* domain truncation to the\n285|  non-NUL prefix, plus the empty-generation bound.\n286|* Signature verification: forged-key and nil-verifier arms both held.\n287|* Evidence tuple/coverage/ID-set: 11/12, including the two narrowing mutants that\n288|  drop only the generation or only the fixture from the tuple comparison, and the\n289|  `factsCover` first-requirement-only mutant.\n290|* The logbook entry is honest about the three things the mutant protocol caught\n291|  during development, including a real production bug (nil-vs-empty slice\n292|  round-trip). That is the right instinct.\n293|\n294|## Stated bounds — what this method cannot see\n295|\n296|* **JCS is unprovable with these fixtures.** `M71` and `M72` (replace\n297|  `jcs.Transform` with the raw `json.Marshal` output) both survive. They survive\n298|  because these three schemas have ASCII-only keys and no numeric members, so\n299|  Go's map marshalling and RFC 8785 agree byte for byte. This is a real property\n300|  of the fixtures, not a defect, and not something a new test can fix without a\n301|  fixture carrying a non-ASCII key or a number — which the closed schemas forbid.\n302|  Report as unknown, not as covered.\n303|* **`M92` (nil-registry check in `AdmitProbe`) and `M95` (absent-vs-null in\n304|  `digestOrNullMember`) are near-equivalent mutants**: `Registry.Resolve` has its\n305|  own nil guard and `checkExactMembers` already guarantees presence, so removing\n306|  either changes the arm but not the outcome. Counted as survivors above for\n307|  honesty; they are not defects.\n308|* Mutation testing bounds the suite, not the code. A gate whose mutant is killed\n309|  is exercised; it is not thereby proven correct against the spec. F1 is the case\n310|  in point — area 14 scores `full` on mutants and the gate is still wrong.\n311|* Coverage-arm mapping uses the max hit count of any block containing the return\n312|  statement, so the 71/121 figure is an upper bound on arms executed.\n313|* Concurrency is out of scope: nothing here is measured under `-race` beyond the\n314|  default suite run.\n315|\n316|## Required for acceptance\n317|\n318|1. Fix F1 — decide `manifest`/`probe` against §4.D (`Capability dependencies:\n319|   none`) and make `CheckOperation` and `CapabilitiesForOperation` agree; add the\n320|   negative test for the operation whose gate must not refuse.\n321|2. Narrow the four F2 fixtures so each is rejected only by the rule its case is\n322|   named for, and assert `*Error.Detail` in every refusal case, not just the wire\n323|   code.\n324|3. Add the F3 executable-substitution case against `Registry.AdmitProbe`.\n325|4. Add the F4 `now == expires_at` row.\n326|5. Close the F5 class: give `ParseProbe` and `ParseEvidence` the same\n327|   schema/schema_version/backend-identity/kind/digest cases the manifest parser\n328|   has.\n329|6. Re-establish G-A over the two claim slice fields, with a probe that re-reads\n330|   the package interior rather than only re-comparing `Admitted`.\n331|7. F9 — correct or re-scope the README `15.3#3` sentence and register this leaf's\n332|   evidence in the ownership projection, as leaf 1 did.\n333|\n334|F6, F7 and F8 are non-blocking but cheap; closing them with the rest is the\n335|sensible move.\n336|\n337|Re-run the whole mutant battery after rework: the survivors above are the\n338|acceptance target, and the two stated-bound survivors (`M71`, `M72`) plus the two\n339|near-equivalents (`M92`, `M95`) are expected to remain."}}}
