{"schema_version":1,"id":"018f0000-0000-7000-8000-00000000d207","stream":{"kind":"session","id":"01a06ee5-ad0e-7750-9534-32e3571b58b2"},"sequence":1886,"recorded_at":1780531400003767,"record_type":"event","durability":"durable","causation_id":"268a8b73-3d5a-46cf-996a-bd1fafa2d2a2","payload_type":"tool.result","payload_schema_version":1,"payload":{"kind":"tool_result","command_id":"268a8b73-3d5a-46cf-996a-bd1fafa2d2a2","run_stream":{"kind":"run","id":"268a8b73-3d5a-46cf-996a-bd1fafa2d2a2"},"call_id":"call_01a06ef202db7b13a42d0a0486db89ee","text":"{\n  \"chunk_id\": \"exec-48-1\",\n  \"command\": \"tail -n 60 LOGBOOK.md; echo \\\"===HEAD===\\\"; head -n 30 LOGBOOK.md\",\n  \"description\": \"Check logbook format\",\n  \"exit_code\": 0,\n  \"terminal_status\": \"completed\",\n  \"output\": \"- ANOMALY: `_defensive=1` in the DSN is honoured by modernc.org/sqlite (`SQLITE_DBCONFIG_DEFENSIVE`) and is the only thing stopping the live connection from rewriting `sqlite_master` — precisely the mutation the drift fixtures perform on raw connections. The word appeared exactly once in the whole change, on that DSN line. Two lines above, `_synchronous` carried a full subsumption note; the parameter that *was* individually observable had none.\\n- DECISION: A refusal test needs a forward control when the refusal could plausibly be the dependency's default. `TestOpenProjectionConnectionRefusesSchemaRewrites` asserts the same two statements *succeed* on a connection opened without `_defensive`, so the refusal is attributed to this projection's configuration rather than to SQLite baseline behaviour.\\n- DECISION: Extract the inline `sort.Slice` closure to `sortIndexedObjects` so the comparator can be driven directly with a same-digest pair. The scan can never produce that input — one digest owns one path under `digest_path_v1` — so the `RelativePath` tie-break was untestable while the comparator stayed inline.\\n- ANOMALY: Deleting the ordering *call* from `scanAuthoritativeBlobs` stays green and legitimately so: `os.ReadDir` returns filename-sorted entries and the shard split makes that traversal already ascending by digest. Genuine subsumption by a dependency's contract rather than by another check in the suite — documented at the call site, with the order itself pinned in both directions on the extracted comparator.\\n- SCOPE: `internal/localstore/projection_test.go` (3 new cases, self-checking fixture, independent fingerprint derivation), `internal/localstore/projection.go` (`sortIndexedObjects` extraction plus two call-site comments), `README.md`.\\n- STATUS: Resolved. 8 mutants, 7 red as required, 1 green as a documented subsumption. TASK-260830-3amrl9 rev8, handed to review.\\n\\n### 2320 — A derived inventory must be derived from the construct, not from a list of construct names\\n- FINDING: A twelve-mutant sweep on this leaf reported \\\"12/12 killed, 0 survivors\\\" and the review then found roughly twenty declared bounds with no negative test at all. Widening `bounded(\\\"terminal_id\\\", 1, 512)` to `1, 4096` — an 8x widening of a declared bound — passed `go test ./...`, both fuzz targets and `tracecheck`. The sweep proved only that the twelve hand-picked gates worked.\\n- ROOT CAUSE: the same meta-defect as the previous entry, one level up. The gate proved its own coverage over *member sets*; nothing proved coverage over *bounds*, and the sweep that would have caught it selected its own mutants by hand.\\n- FIX: `internal/canonicaljson/declared_bounds_test.go` derives the bound-helper set transitively from the package sources (a function with a bounded `name` and an int `minimum`/`maximum`, plus any wrapper forwarding that name with literal bounds), turns every call site into one obligation per declared direction, and asserts the obligation set equals the proof set in both directions. Because the obligation key carries the literal bound, widening a bound reddens the suite *before any case runs*. 58 call sites across 57 distinct keys, 98 obligation instances.\\n- DECISION: two obligations are unreachable at a public entry and are declared, not waved through — the 65,536-entry `GitIndex` bound (refused by the outer 5 MiB size gate first) and a 256-entry nested submodule array (refused by the whole-tree submodule count first). Each names its subsuming refusal and a test that must exist in the package, and both sets are asserted exactly.\\n- FINDING: a mechanically derived sweep over the 135 bound arguments and *entry-level* refusal guards in `core_records.go` found 8 survivors the hand-picked sweep never generated — every refusal `ValidateObservationEvent` and `ValidateObservationStream` emit before the event shape is reached (decode failure, non-AX number, non-object, invalid member event, empty stream) plus the `requireArrayMinimum` type guard. All now covered.\\n- CORRECTION (2026-09-02, revision 4): the sentence above originally read \\\"all 135 bound arguments and refusal guards in `core_records.go`\\\" and \\\"All now covered\\\". That does not reproduce and overstated the sweep. What that sweep enumerated was every *bound argument* plus the guards whose condition it could rewrite to `false`; it was not the refusal-guard set. A statement-coverage profile of the same tree shows **91 runtime refusal branches in `core_records.go` that no test executed once**, at 87.7% package coverage, and the round-3 review deleted eight normative gates simultaneously with `go test ./...` still fully green. The correct claim for revision 3 is: a derived sweep over bound arguments found 8 survivors among the mutants it generated, and said nothing about the refusal-guard set.\\n- FINDING: the last survivor is an equivalent mutant, not a gap: `requireArrayMinimum`'s absent-member branch is unreachable because its only call site reads `predecessors` after `requireExactMembers` has already refused an omission. Pinned by name rather than deleted.\\n- ANOMALY: `Lease Record` carried an invented constraint. Section 5.3 declares `predecessor_lease_id` \\\"Null only at epoch 1\\\", \\\"An epoch-1 `create` lease MUST have a null predecessor\\\", and `checkpoint_id` \\\"Null only for epoch-1 `create`\\\". It nowhere declares that epoch 1 implies `reason = create`, and that inference refused a legitimate epoch-1 `recovery` lease. Removed both inferred directions; the checkpoint rule now correctly covers the whole complement of epoch-1-`create`, which the old epoch-only form missed. Third invented-constraint finding across the m0 leaves.\\n- NOTE: `validateProviderIdentityRecord` ranged a Go map to apply three bounds, so a record violating several reported a random member — and the bounds were invisible to any call-site derivation. Spelled out as three ordered calls.\\n- SCOPE: `internal/canonicaljson/`, `internal/traceability/ownership.v0.5.0.json`, `README.md`\\n- STATUS: Resolved; TASK-260830-1tax26 handed to review as revision 3.\\n\\n### 2300 — Sibling terms hide a clause the same way preceding lines do\\n- ROOT CAUSE: `equalProjectionSchemaEntry` in `internal/localstore/projection.go` compares four identity fields in one `||` chain. Every existing drift fixture changed the SQL text or the `(type, name)` key, so a sibling term always decided the outcome first and the `actual.table != expected.table` term was reachable, exercised and pinned by nothing. Deleting only that term left the entire `localstore` suite green — reproduced before fixing.\\n- FINDING: This is the round-5 shape-clause defect in a different syntactic shape. There the check that decided instead of the target was the *next line*; here it is a *sibling term in the same expression*. The isolation rule generalises: a negative fixture must leave every other decider matching, whether that decider sits above the target or beside it.\\n- FINDING: SQLite accepts `PRAGMA writable_schema=ON; UPDATE sqlite_master SET tbl_name='projection_metadata' WHERE type='index' AND sql IS NULL` and reopens the database. Every other sqlite_master rewrite tried in this package fails as `malformed database schema`. That makes the moved autoindex `tbl_name` the one drift class no other comparison term can observe, and the only fixture that isolates this term.\\n- DECISION: Extend `readProjectionSchemaShape` with per-field maps (`names`, `tables`, `hasStatement`) rather than reuse its `keys` field. `keys` embeds the table name, so it cannot express \\\"this term changed and those did not\\\" — the assertion the anti-vacuity guard needs.\\n- DECISION: Verify a reviewer's subsumption argument instead of quoting it. Both `hasStatement` drift directions were re-probed here; the two `SQLITE_CORRUPT` messages reproduce verbatim, but only when the raw reopen is taken *before* production recovery runs — my first probe read the already-rebuilt database and reported a clean open for both.\\n- ANOMALY: A green mutant is not evidence of subsumption unless the ordering is checked. `Objects()` size bounds survive deletion because `verifyProjectionSchema` runs inside `openProjection` before any `*Projection` is returned; without that ordering the same green result would mean the bound is simply untested.\\n- SCOPE: `internal/localstore/projection_test.go` (new tbl_name drift case, near-miss `sha256-old` objects-root subtest, extended shape helper), `internal/localstore/projection.go` (comments only — verified against the rev6 patch, 44 raw diff lines, 0 after stripping comment lines), `README.md`.\\n- STATUS: Resolved. 6 mutants, 3 red as required, 3 green as documented subsumptions. TASK-260830-3amrl9 rev7, handed to review.\\n\\n### 2245 — Reaching a refusal clause is not pinning it\\n- ROOT CAUSE: Every fixture for a *shape* clause in `internal/localstore/projection.go` — the checks that refuse a path because it is not the kind of file it must be — was a directory at mode `0700` or a symlink at lstat permission `0777`. Both are refused by the `verifyOwnerFileInfo(info, 0o600)` call on the following line, so the shape clause was reached but never decided anything the suite could observe. Five clauses survived individual disablement; the blob-leaf pair survived being disabled *together*.\\n- FINDING: A derived refusal inventory can prove a site was reached. It cannot prove the site decided the outcome. Those are different facts, and positive-path-only coverage of a refusal looks identical to real coverage in both the inventory and the mutant count until the fixture is built so that every preceding check passes.\\n- FINDING: The isolating fixture for this whole family is one thing — a FIFO created at exactly mode `0600`. Not a symlink, owned by the effective user, already owner-only, so only the shape clause can refuse it. It had been written once, for the lock file, and not applied to any sibling.\\n- ANOMALY: The blob-leaf regularity clause bounds availability, not only safety. `os.Open` and SQLite block indefinitely on a FIFO with no writer, so removing the clause converts a refusal into an unbounded hang inside `OpenProjection`. A negative case that calls the entry point inline would hang the suite instead of reddening it; the case must assert a bounded return. Mutant `m11` reddens after 20.04s at that bound.\\n- DECISION: Where an isolating fixture exists, pin the clause rather than annotate it `projection-refusal-subsumed`. The shard-kind clause was provably subsumed by `verifyOwnerDirectory` and was still worth pinning with a 0600 FIFO shard, because subsumption is an argument and a fixture is evidence.\\n- DECISION: `unix.Flock` mutual exclusion was advertised in `README.md` and pinned by nothing — the 6-worker convergence test passes on SQLite's `busy_timeout` alone. flock arbitrates *open file descriptions*, so holding one in-process reproduces a second process exactly; no subprocess needed.\\n- SCOPE: `internal/localstore/projection_unix_test.go` (8 new cases, bounded-open helper, fixture-isolation assertion), `README.md`. No production source changed — every round-5 finding was an evidence defect, not a behaviour defect.\\n- STATUS: Resolved. Full sweep of both projection-owned sources: 34 mutants, 34 red, no survivors, including two forward controls on the inventory gate. TASK-260830-3amrl9 rev6, handed to review.\\n\\n### 2129 — Derived-completeness gate blind to its own refusal class\\n- ROOT CAUSE: `internal/localstore/projection_refusal_inventory_test.go` derived expected refusal sites from the production AST but matched only `*ast.Ident` calls named `projectionRefusal`. Six live clauses written as bare `fmt.Errorf(\\\"%w: ...\\\", ErrUnsafeOwnership)` or as delegated `verifyOwnerFileInfo`/`verifyOwnerDirectory` guards were invisible to the gate whose job was to notice them, and shipped unpinned while 20+ funnelled sites were enforced.\\n- FINDING: A completeness gate over a *pattern* must also police the pattern. Deriving the expected set is not enough if the extraction rule silently excludes a whole syntactic class.\\n- FIX: Second funnel `projectionOwnershipRefusal` (`internal/localstore/projection.go:117`) keeps the refusal decision at its call site while preserving the owner-only cause; every projection refusal rerouted through a funnel. Gate now derives two properties — funnel sites need an exercised negative path beneath `openProjection`, AND no projection-owned source may raise a refusal outside a funnel. Build-context filtering added so a platform-specific refusal is not demanded from a platform that cannot run it.\\n- ANOMALY: I reproduced the identical bug class inside the fix. The new unrouted check sat behind `typed.Fun.(*ast.Ident)`, and `fmt.Errorf` is a `*ast.SelectorExpr`, so the exact class stayed invisible in code written specifically to catch it. First run of mutant m41 exited 0. Reading the new gate would not have found this; only the behaviour-preserving mutant did.\\n- DECISION: Assume \\\"unreachable\\\" last, not first. One clause presumed unreachable — the non-regular branch of `ensureProjectionFileModes` — is drivable through the real production entry by replacing a sidecar with a FIFO in the post-rebuild hook. Only three clauses are genuine TOCTOU re-checks, each annotated with the earlier check that owns the reachable case.\\n- SCOPE: `internal/localstore/projection.go`, `projection_lock_unix.go`, `projection_refusal_inventory_test.go`, new `projection_unix_test.go`, `README.md`.\\n- STATUS: Resolved. 15 mutants, 13 red as required, 1 designed control green, 1 documented subsumed green. TASK-260830-3amrl9 rev5, handed to review.\\n\\n### 2113 — A gate must prove its own coverage; so must a mutation sweep\\n- ROOT CAUSE: `requireMemberSet` was a byte-identical duplicate of `requireExactMembers`. `TestConstraintEnumerationMatchesRequireExactMembers` scanned only the latter by name, so `WorkspaceMember.*`, every Session Event payload, `Observation Event`, and `ObservationCounts` carried zero per-member enumeration rows while the README claimed a complete inventory.\\n- FINDING: That blind spot is why a real defect reached review — `internal/canonicaljson/core_records.go` `validateTerminalV4Payload` validated `terminal_backend_id` with `requireString` only, so `CalculateObjectIdentity` attested `AX.TMUX`, `../../etc/passwd`, an embedded NUL and 5000 chars. SPEC.md:890 (§4.B) pins it to 1–128 ASCII bytes matching `[a-z][a-z0-9]*(?:[.-][a-z0-9]+)*`.\\n- FIX: Duplicate deleted, all 5 call sites collapsed onto `requireExactMembers`. Grammar enforced by `requireTerminalBackendID`. `TestRequireExactMembersIsTheOnlyClosedMemberGate` now derives every function emitting a closed-member refusal and fails unless that set is exactly `{requireExactMembers}`; the scanned file set is derived from the package directory, not a hand-written list.\\n- ANOMALY: The mutation harness aborted mid-run on the zsh reserved variable `status`, after applying mutant 1 but before its restore. The next run took its baseline backup from that mutated tree, so all 12 mutants were behavioural no-ops against an already-red mask and every one reported KILLED for the wrong reason. Caught only because the full-repo run afterwards failed.\\n- FIX: Harness refuses to sweep unless the baseline is green, and reports `NOT-APPLIED` for any mutant that left the tree unchanged. Re-run: 12/12 killed, 0 survivors.\\n- DECISION: Session Event payload members are pinned by mechanical extraction from the pinned SPEC's four `Exact payload members` tables into `internal/canonicaljson/testdata/session-event-payload-members.md` (39 event types, 204 declarations) rather than hand-listed across 134 version×event shapes.\\n- FINDING: The first extractor split on `</code>,` and silently dropped members followed by a bare `(G2)` annotation, losing `clone.planned.projection_plan_id` and `clone.failed.materialization_id`. The new derivation test caught it; review had not. Parse one `<code>` span per declaration.\\n- NOTE: General property, applies to every derived gate — an inventory must assert that the set of constructs it scans is complete for the package, and a mutation sweep must assert its baseline is green. A gate that cannot fail when someone adds a second copy of a check is decoration, and a mutant that \\\"kills\\\" an already-red mask proves nothing.\\n- SCOPE: `internal/canonicaljson/`, `internal/traceability/ownership.v0.5.0.json`, `README.md`\\n- STATUS: Resolved; TASK-260830-1tax26 handed to review as revision 2.\\n===HEAD===\\n# Flight Logbook\\n\\n> Institutional memory. Concise, factual, high-signal.\\n> Newest entries first. One block per insight.\\n\\n## 2026-09-04\\n\\n### 2340 — Terminal manifest/probe/capability-evidence admission (leaf 2): mutant protocol caught a mis-targeted expiry test\\n\\n- SCOPE: TASK-260830-1zvmw7 on leaf-1 checkpoint `9481a20`. New: `internal/terminalbackend/manifest.go` (closed Manifest/Probe/Evidence schemas, 16-row capability registry, generation digest, rule 1–6 reconciliation, expiry, signature-verifier seam, operation gate, `Registry.AdmitProbe`); tests `manifest_test.go` (black-box, independent JCS/identity/RSA-signing recipe) + `manifest_pin_test.go` (white-box for parse-unreachable arms). Leaf-1 files touched only for a doc line and 3 added wire codes; no gate logic changed.\\n- MUTANT PROTOCOL CAUGHT A MIS-TARGETED TEST: the \\\"expired evidence\\\" case re-signed mutated evidence (new ID) without refreshing the probe's `evidence_ids`, so under the original code it failed at liveness first (right arm, wrong reason to trust), and under a neutered-liveness mutant it failed at the ID-set arm instead of admitting — the test passed for the wrong reason and the mutant survived. Fix: every evidence-mutating case now calls `finalizeProbeIDs` after re-signing (6 cases + platform case), so each refusal lands on its intended arm; the corrected liveness mutant is killed.\\n- DETERMINISTIC SIGNATURES DEDUP: RSA PKCS1v15 is deterministic per key+message, so a \\\"distinct extra evidence\\\" fixture built with identical fields was byte-identical and correctly deduplicated — the test was wrong, production right. Unreferenced-object case now varies a supplementary fact (`ui_absent`) to force distinct bytes; identical-duplicate acceptance is pinned white-box as the stated bound.\\n- REAL PRODUCTION BUG FOUND BY TEST: strict decoder returned nil (not empty) slices for `[]`, so `json.Marshal` re-encoded them as `null` and omit-self identity recomputation diverged on claim-free/evidence-free documents. Fixed with non-nil array initialization; pinned by the empty-tuple admission test.\\n- EVIDENCE: board outcome artifact `TASK-260830-1zvmw7_manifest-probe-evidence.md` — 12/12 mutants killed (each grep-confirmed present, compiling, red, reverted byte-identical), suite/vet/gofmt/tracecheck exit 0, package coverage 87.1%.\\n\\n### 2210 — Terminal backend registry rework: aliasing bypass closed, four unmeasured domains measured\\n\\n- SCOPE: rework of TASK-260830-2xdt8t after CHANGES REQUESTED (five blocking findings F1–F5). Production: `internal/terminalbackend` clones slices on ingress (`New` per-builtin copy, `RegisterExternal` store copy) and egress (`Resolve` return copy). No new refusal sites.\\n- F1: `Resolve` returned the struct by value with shared backing arrays, `RegisterExternal` stored the caller record verbatim, `New` shared one protocol array across both built-ins — an in-place mutation converted drift into `terminal_backend_ambiguous` and the registry adopted the change. Pinned by `TestRegistryCopiesSlicesAcrossItsBoundary` (four subtests, incl. the end-to-end duplicate-instead-of-drift bypass).\\n- F2: drift table gains same-length cases (`[\\\"1.0.0\\\"]→[\\\"1.1.0\\\"]`, `[linux]→[macos]`); the length-changing cases alone left the member loops deletable.\\n- F3: generation-bound test rewritten with EQUAL generations on both sides (1 and 256 admitted; 0 and 257 refused); mismatch arm split into `TestCheckProviderDescriptorGenerationMismatch`. The old test proved the inequality, not the bound.\\n- F4: `validatePlatforms` fully narrowed (dup/empty/unknown/5-list, with the 5-list asserting the `platforms bound` clause since any 5-list over the 4-member vocabulary also fails a later rule); protocol `[1..32]` bound pinned with 33 distinct sorted members plus 32-member admission; `DigestFile` FIFO case pins the `IsRegular` guard (the directory case is EISDIR-confounded); trust-loop `ParseID` pinned against deletion by the `INVALID_ID` wiring case.\\n- EQUIVALENT MUTANTS (documented, not covered): reverting the trust-loop `ParseID` to the previous local pattern is behavior-identical — same refusal set, intentionally non-distinguishing clause, `ax.` difference subsumed by the `HasPrefix` line two statements later. `parseKind`'s vocabulary arm and the digest-must-be-null arm are unreachable through current entry points (external-kind gate precedes validation with `Untrusted`, pinned by `TestRegisterExternalRefusesUnknownKindAsUntrusted`); measured white-box in `internal_pin_test.go` with the reachability bound stated.\\n- F5: `TestDecodeRefusesNonConptyBackendOnNativeWindows` registered in `config-versioned-readers`; ownership projection digest re-pinned; README discharge prose names both arms. No figure moved (74 cases, 17/403).\\n- EVIDENCE: `TASK-260830-2xdt8t_rework-verification.md` (board outcome artifact) with the mutation re-run ratio.\\n\\n### 2147 — Terminal backend registry: the two board-disclosed findings decided without inventing constraints\\n\\n- SCOPE: new `internal/terminalbackend/` (registry, canonical IDs, version tuples, trust, duplicate/drift refusal, restore binding, §7.A descriptor check), `internal/config/validation.go` wiring, new `internal/config/terminal_registry_wiring_test.go`. TASK-260830-2xdt8t, spec v0.5.0 §4.B, §4.1, §6.5, §7.A. Signed commit on story branch, worktree clean, not pushed.\\n- DECISION (finding 1): `required_capabilities` keeps its empty default. SPEC.md:2585 (\\\"default platform lane minimum\\\") is the sole occurrence of the phrase in the 12,665-line SPEC; no per-platform minimum set is enumerated anywhere, so implementing one would invent a constraint. STATED BOUND (review correction to this entry's earlier reasoning): the clause is underspecified, so empty is the only non-inventing default — and the choice is recorded as underspecification, not as a neutral default, because `required_capabilities` restricts and empty is therefore the weaker gate. The earlier appeal to SPEC.md:2604-2605 did not carry the argument and is withdrawn. No code change; audit finding recorded as ANSWERED.\\n\",\n  \"original_output_bytes\": 22621,\n  \"original_output_tokens\": 5656,\n  \"truncated\": false\n}","correlation_facts":{"tool_name":"bash","outcome":"success"}}}
